          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Dew primarily signifies God-given blessing, favor, refreshment, and life-giving grace; divine teaching or speech is a recognized secondary, context-specific meaning.\",\"book_summary\":\"Dew is identified primarily with the word descending quietly from heaven, linked to manna as daily bread and to the possibility of receiving the word only temporarily.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Divine blessing and life-giving refreshment.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The word or divine speech descending from heaven.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus confines the word/teaching sense chiefly to Deuteronomy 32:2 while treating blessing and refreshment as the dominant category. The book elevates the word to the governing identity and reclassifies the manna, nourishment, and evaporation motifs around that identification.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"The book replaces the consensus's primary referent—divine blessing or refreshing grace—with divine speech or the word. Although the word can convey blessing, the two are not equivalent symbolic identifications, and the book's own common-view note marks the distinction.\",\"rationale\":\"This is not NOVEL because all consensus sets recognize God's word, teaching, or doctrine as an established dew interpretation. It is nevertheless DIVERGENT from the consensus core because the book promotes that secondary referent to the primary identity rather than merely narrowing which forms of blessing are intended. Some scriptural cases overlap, especially Deuteronomy 32:2 and the manna passages, so the extensions are partially reclassified rather than wholly disjoint.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Deuteronomy 32:2 directly compares divine speech to dew, and the manna texts combined with Deuteronomy 8:3 support the word-as-bread association; therefore the book could be viewed as selecting a recognized contextual subtype of divine blessing rather than contradicting it. However, the printed citations do not directly establish that this is dew's general identity, and they do not clearly support the added 'without root' and evaporation claim.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}"
        }
